+# BLOCKED: `002_unique_email.sql` cannot be applied to the production data
+
+## Summary
+
+`users.email` cannot be made `NOT NULL` and case-insensitively unique without
+changing or deleting existing customer rows. The production snapshot contains
+both a `NULL` email and case-insensitive duplicates, and Support owns that data.
+No SQL-only migration can enforce the requested invariants while leaving every
+customer row untouched, so this migration is blocked on a Support data decision.
+
+## What blocks it
+
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` has 40 users. Two independent problems:
+
+1. **A required email does not exist for every row.** User `id = 17`
+   (`Goran Okafor`) has `email IS NULL`, so `NOT NULL` cannot hold.
+2. **Emails are not case-insensitively unique.** Grouping by `lower(email)`
+   finds a collision:
+
+   ```
+   ana.silva@example.com -> ids 1, 4, 23
+   ```
+
+   - `id = 1`: `ana.silva@example.com`
+   - `id = 4`: `Ana.Silva@example.com` (name `Dev Silva`)
+   - `id = 23`: `ana.silva@example.com` (exact duplicate of id 1)
+
+SQLite cannot add `NOT NULL` to an existing column in place
+(`ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN email SET NOT NULL` fails), so a real migration
+must rebuild `users` and copy every row into the constrained table. That copy
+fails on this snapshot:
+
+- at `id = 4`, with `UNIQUE constraint failed`
+- at `id = 17`, with `NOT NULL constraint failed`
+
+The only ways to make it apply are to update/delete/merge rows (forbidden), or
+to grandfather the invalid rows (a workaround, rejected below).
+
+## Approaches considered and rejected
+
+- **`UPDATE` / `DELETE` / merge the offending rows.** Directly violates the
+  requirement that the migration must not modify or delete customer rows, and
+  the policy (which duplicate is canonical, what to do with id 17) belongs to
+  Support.
+- **`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON users(lower(email)) WHERE email IS NOT NULL`.**
+  Still fails on ids 1/4/23, and a partial index does not make email required.
+- **Triggers that reject new bad writes.** Existing NULL/duplicate rows stay,
+  the column is still nullable and the table still has no unique constraint.
+  This silently makes the old data exempt, which is not "become required and
+  unique" and is exactly the kind of workaround that was ruled out.
+- **Conditional no-op when the data is dirty.** The migration would report
+  success while enforcing nothing, hiding the problem instead of surfacing it.
+- **`PRAGMA writable_schema` edits.** Rewrites schema text without real
+  enforcement and risks database corruption.
+
+## What was implemented
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` contains the correct target schema: a
+rebuilt `users` table with `email TEXT NOT NULL` and a unique index on
+`lower(email)`, copying `id`, `email` and `name` verbatim. It starts with a
+preflight check that aborts with
+
+```
+CHECK constraint failed: users_email_must_be_present_and_unique
+```
+
+before any schema change if the existing rows cannot satisfy the constraints.
+On the production snapshot it therefore fails without touching any row: all 40
+rows and the original schema are unchanged after the abort.
+
+Test status:
+
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` **passes** (NOT NULL and case-insensitive
+  uniqueness are enforced).
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` **fails by design** at the preflight. It
+  cannot pass without either changing customer rows or weakening the
+  constraint, both of which are out of bounds.
+
+There is no implementation that makes both tests green under the stated
+constraints. A green `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` requires either that the
+migration changes/deletes the four offending rows, or that it skips/weakens
+enforcement when the data is dirty (partial index, triggers, conditional
+no-op). The former is forbidden, and the latter is the workaround this task
+rules out; it would leave production without the requested invariant.
+
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` and `test_migrations.py` were not modified.
+
+## What unblocks it
+
+Support needs to decide and apply:
+
+1. **`id = 17`**: supply the real email, or remove/anonymise the row per their
+   policy.
+2. **`ids 1, 4, 23`**: choose the canonical account and merge/delete the
+   others. Note that `id = 4` is `Ana.Silva@example.com` but is named
+   `Dev Silva`, so it may be a mistyped email rather than a real duplicate.
+
+Once the snapshot has no `NULL` emails and no `lower(email)` duplicates, the
+existing `002_unique_email.sql` can be applied unchanged; it will copy the
+rows verbatim and add the constraints. No code change is needed.
diff --git a/migrations/002_unique_email.sql b/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
index 91e11a9..9b2580a 100644
--- a/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
+++ b/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
@@ -1,2 +1,34 @@
 -- 002: users.email must be present and unique, ignoring case.
--- TODO
+--
+-- BLOCKED against the production snapshot: see BLOCKED.md. This migration
+-- refuses to run while existing rows would have to be changed or deleted to
+-- satisfy the constraints. It does not touch any customer row in that case.
+
+-- Preflight: abort before any schema change if the existing data cannot
+-- satisfy NOT NULL + unique lower(email). The constraint name is the error.
+DROP TABLE IF EXISTS temp.email_migration_precheck;
+CREATE TEMP TABLE email_migration_precheck (
+    clean INTEGER NOT NULL
+        CONSTRAINT users_email_must_be_present_and_unique CHECK (clean = 1)
+);
+INSERT INTO email_migration_precheck (clean)
+SELECT CASE WHEN
+    EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM users WHERE email IS NULL)
+    OR EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM users GROUP BY lower(email) HAVING count(*) > 1)
+THEN 0 ELSE 1 END;
+DROP TABLE email_migration_precheck;
+
+-- SQLite cannot add NOT NULL to an existing column, so rebuild the table and
+-- copy every row verbatim (same ids, emails and names).
+BEGIN;
+CREATE TABLE users_new (
+    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
+    email TEXT NOT NULL,
+    name TEXT
+);
+INSERT INTO users_new (id, email, name)
+    SELECT id, email, name FROM users;
+DROP TABLE users;
+ALTER TABLE users_new RENAME TO users;
+CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_nocase ON users(lower(email));
+COMMIT;
